<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lingga Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lingga Archipelago</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lingga Islands are an archipelago south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-archipelago">Riau Islands</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, centred on Lingga Island. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a> they formed, with the Riau Islands, the Dutch-protected Riau-Lingga Sultanate, whose sultans resided at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daik">Daik</a> on Lingga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lingga-islands.jpg" alt="Lingga Islands" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dheerav at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Singkep_38_jago.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…is generally thought to have been revised for the Johor court in 1612, and the Bugis-Malay court of Riau-<b>Lingga</b> became a centre of Malay letters in the nineteenth century, producing Raja Ali Haji's Tuhfat al-Nafis and grammars that helped standardise the Malay language from which modern Malay and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The mainland and Singapore fell in the British sphere, while the Riau and <b>Lingga</b> islands stayed with the Dutch under a separate line of sultans based at Daik on Lingga, descended from the Bendahara dynasty of Johor.</blockquote><a href="/state/riau-lingga-sultanate">Riau-Lingga Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
